<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akademgorodok</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1957">1957</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akademgorodok is a scientific town near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novosibirsk">Novosibirsk</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, founded in 1957 as the centre of the new Siberian Branch of the USSR Academy of Sciences. Built in forest beside the Ob reservoir, it housed dozens of research institutes and Novosibirsk State University. It became a symbol of Soviet scientific ambition and of the relatively open intellectual climate of the Khrushchev years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akademgorodok.jpg" alt="Akademgorodok" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Minsvyaz.ru · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Novosibirsk_Technopark,_2_October_2014.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Akademgorodok, Russian for Academic Town, is part of the Sovetsky District of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novosibirsk">Novosibirsk</a>. It lies 30 km south of the city centre and about 10 km west of Koltsovo, in birch and pine forest on the shore of the Ob Sea, an artificial reservoir on the river Ob. Formally it belongs to Novosibirsk and has never been a closed city.</p><p>Besides Novosibirsk State University it holds research institutes in fields from nuclear physics, catalysis and mathematics to cytology, genetics, history and philology, a medical academy, housing and community amenities. The House of Scientists, its social centre, has a library of 100 thousand volumes, a picture gallery, lecture halls and a concert hall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was founded in 1957 under the auspices of the Academy of Sciences of the USSR. Academician Mikhail Alexeyevich Lavrentyev, a mechanician and mathematician and the first chairman of the Siberian Branch of the Academy, played a prominent part in establishing it. At its peak it was home to 65,000 scientists and their families and was a privileged place to live. Residents had access to special food distribution outlets, scientists with a doctorate to a doctoral food delivery service, and full and corresponding members of the Academy to a still higher level of service and to single-family houses called cottages, which were considered luxurious by Soviet standards.</p><p>In the early years residents enjoyed great freedom from the rules of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. A modernist cultural centre exhibited works by banned Soviet artists, poetry evenings were held, and research in areas dismissed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> as dangerous pseudoscience, such as cybernetics and genetics, flourished. These freedoms were severely curtailed in the 1970s, in the Brezhnev era.</p><p>After the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> many scientists were reduced to penury. From the mid-1990s private investment began to arrive: the software company Novosoft, whose chief client was IBM, was founded in 1992, and CFT, which specialises in banking software, started about then. Private investment reached US$10 million by 1997, $150 million by 2006 and about $1 billion by 2015. Intel and Schlumberger also brought work to the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Akademgorodok is sometimes called the Silicon Forest or Silicon Taiga. By 2015, 300 companies had been set up there since 2011, employing about 9,000 people. Its Technopark, with a distinctive headquarters built in 2013 of two inclined towers joined by a passage on the 13th and 14th floors, is nicknamed the geese.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Akademgorodok” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
